    "stdout_excerpt": "COUNTERFACT_JSON={\"expected\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"observed\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"reason\": \"the refusal is raised by InsiderTradingFetcher.transform_data in opendata/data/providers/tmx/models/fetchers_w33_graphql.py, at the if not isinstance(transactions, list) guard inside the buy/sell flatten -- the site this leg's traceback ends in. The operation publishes each period as one object with exactly two direction arrays, and the mapper takes the direction from the array a record sat in because no cell names it, so a period whose buy array is gone is a reply that cannot be read as transactions at all. Dropping it is the tamper that reaches that guard: the second period and the activitySummary both stay intact, so the count, list_rows and the envelope check all pass, and without the guard the flatten would iterate None and the caller would get a bare TypeError carrying neither a code nor a field attribution; untampered control produced 4 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
  },
